1
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:23,900
Many chemistry and biochemistry procedures will require you to perform a reaction at

2
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,580
a specific pH.

3
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:29,820
To accomplish this task, you must understand how to both make a buffer and how to use

4
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:31,640
a pH meter.

5
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:36,100
This video will demonstrate two methods of buffer preparation as well as proper use of

6
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:39,260
the pH meter.

7
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:44,820
A buffer is an ionic compound that resists changes to its pH when strong acids or bases

8
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:47,680
are added to it.

9
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:52,680
The amount of strong acid or strong base that can be neutralized by a particular buffer

10
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,400
is dictated by the buffer's capacity, which is determined by the concentration of weak

11
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,820
acid and weak base in the buffer.

12
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:05,580
A higher concentration of buffering reagents translates into greater buffering capacity.

13
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:11,100
In the first part of this video, we will walk through two methods of buffer preparation.

14
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:16,800
In the first example, you will need to prepare one liter of a 50 millimolar buffer for a

15
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,760
protein that is stable at pH 7.3.

16
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,320
The first step is choosing an appropriate buffer system.

17
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:29,160
This buffer system should produce a stable pH in the range needed and should have a capacity

18
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,160
large enough to accommodate your solutions.

19
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:37,400
The weak acid used for the system must have a pKa that is plus or minus one unit of

20
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,800
the desired pH, the closer the better.

21
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:47,520
From this abbreviated list of weak acids, the phosphate ion with a pKa of 6.82 is cruelly

22
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:51,720
the best choice for a buffer with a desired pH of 7.3.

23
00:01:51,720 --> 00:02:00,720
Therefore, the weak acid used for this system is H2PO4-, and the conjugate base is HPO42-,

24
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:07,240
which correspond to the salts sodium phosphate monobasic and sodium phosphate dibasic.

25
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:12,400
After determining an appropriate buffer system, the Henderson-Hasselbach equation is used to

26
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:18,320
calculate the ratio of acid to conjugate base needed at your desired pH.

27
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:24,280
If your desired pH of 7.3 and the pKa of the phosphate ion are substituted into this

28
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:29,880
equation, then the ratio of conjugate base to acid is calculated to be 1.6.

29
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:35,680
Therefore, your buffer must contain 1.6 times the amount of sodium phosphate dibasic to

30
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,200
sodium phosphate monobasic.

31
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:44,680
After determining the appropriate ratio of conjugate base to acid, the exact amount of each reagent

32
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,560
is calculated in the following way.

33
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:52,280
Because you are planning to make one liter of a 50 millimolar phosphate buffer at pH

34
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:58,560
7.3, 50 millimolar is equal to the concentration of acid plus the concentration of conjugate

35
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:00,120
base.

36
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:06,040
In addition, you already know that the ratio of base to acid is 1.6, so the concentration

37
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:10,680
of base is equal to 1.6 times the concentration of acid.

38
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:16,440
By substituting equation 2 into equation 1, you can see that the concentration of acid

39
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:22,440
plus 1.6 times the concentration of acid is equal to 50 millimolar.

40
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:27,080
Through manipulation of this equation, the concentration of acid needed for this particular

41
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:34,440
buffer is 19.2 millimolar and the concentration of base is 30.8 millimolar, which corresponds

42
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:41,360
to 2.30 and 4.37 grams respectively for a one liter solution.

43
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:48,560
To make the buffer, simply dissolve 2.30 grams of sodium phosphate monobasic and 4.37 grams

44
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:53,200
of sodium phosphate dibasic in one liter of distilled water.

45
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:58,240
An alternative method of buffer preparation is simple, accurate, and requires much less

46
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,680
math.

47
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:05,720
To demonstrate this second method, we will prepare 1 liter of a 1.0 millimolar Tris buffer

48
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:08,840
at pH 7.60.

49
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:14,280
The first step involves the determination of the amount of weak acid or in this example

50
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:17,040
weak base needed in the system.

51
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:25,440
To make 1 liter of a 1.0 millimolar Tris buffer, 1.0 millimoles or 121 milligrams of Tris are

52
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:31,480
required at approximately 800 milliliters of distilled water as well as a stir bar to

53
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:32,480
the beaker.

54
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:38,560
Then, weigh 121 milligrams of Tris and transfer it to a 1 liter beaker.

55
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:42,760
Ensure that all of the material has been transferred by rinsing the whey boat and

56
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:45,760
transferring the rinses into the beaker.

57
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:51,640
While stirring, monitor the pH of this solution and add a sodium hydroxide solution drop-wise

58
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:55,240
until the pH is exactly 7.60.

59
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:59,160
The concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution doesn't really matter.

60
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:03,920
However, a solution that is too dilute may require an excessive volume to be added to

61
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:08,520
the buffer, while a solution that is too concentrated may cause you to overshoot the

62
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:11,320
desired pH.

63
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:16,920
If you do happen to exceed the desired pH, then add a hydrochloric acid solution to bring

64
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:19,520
the pH back down.

65
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:24,600
Transfer this solution to a 1 liter volumetric flask, wash the beaker with distilled water

66
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:29,400
several times, and add these washings to the volumetric flask.

67
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:34,160
Dilute the buffer to the mark on the volumetric flask and mix the solution by inverting the

68
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:36,800
flask several times.

69
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:41,120
For the second method of buffer preparation, you needed a pH meter.

70
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:45,400
The final part of this video will discuss the proper use of this equipment.

71
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:52,120
A pH meter allows the determination of hydrogen ion activity by a potentiometric measurement

72
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:58,280
using a glass electrode coaxially joined to a silver-silver chloride reference electrode.

73
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:02,320
When the probe is immersed in solution, the reference electrode makes contact with

74
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,760
the sample, which completes the electrical contact between the reference electrode, sample,

75
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:09,400
and pH electrode.

76
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:14,040
If the unknown solution has a different pH from the solution in the probe, an electrical

77
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:17,720
potential results, which is registered on the meter.

78
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:23,400
This video will demonstrate the use of an Orion SA 520 pH meter.

79
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:28,360
The important components of a pH meter include the display, the probe, and the electrode

80
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:29,680
tip.

81
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,000
The electrode tip contains a fragile glass bulb.

82
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,840
Be extremely careful not to hit the probe on the side of a beaker and when using stir

83
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:39,120
bars.

84
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:45,160
To operate the pH meter, first make sure that the electrode is connected to the meter.

85
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:47,320
Then calibrate the pH meter.

86
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:52,400
The pH meter must be recalibrated every time you turn it on and after three to four hours

87
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,000
of use.

88
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,520
We will perform a two-point calibration.

89
00:06:56,520 --> 00:07:02,520
This method involves calibrating the pH meter with two different buffers of known pH.

90
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:08,680
Turn on the instrument and select the pH mode as 0.1 or 0.01.

91
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:14,640
Then press the ISO button and verify that the pH reads 7.00.

92
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:19,280
Rinse off the probe with distilled water into a waste beaker and gently blot it with a

93
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:21,120
Kimwipe.

94
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:24,560
Place the probe into the first buffer and press the CAL button.

95
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:27,560
The number one should appear on the display.

96
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:32,240
The display will alternate between one and the pH value of the solution.

97
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:36,020
Wait until the display stabilizes and press enter.

98
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,980
The number two should then appear on the display.

99
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:43,500
Rinse the probe with distilled water, blot it with a Kimwipe and place it into the second

100
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:44,660
buffer.

101
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:49,460
If your solution has an acidic pH, then use the 4.01 buffer.

102
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:53,620
If it has a basic pH, use the 10.01 buffer.

103
00:07:53,620 --> 00:07:58,100
The display will alternate between two and the pH value of the solution.

104
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:01,740
Wait until the display stabilizes and press enter.

105
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:08,660
The letters pH will be displayed, indicating that the pH meter is now ready to take measurements.

106
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:12,780
Before taking a measurement, make sure that your sample buffer is at the same temperature

107
00:08:12,780 --> 00:08:17,700
you'll be using in your experiments because pH is temperature dependent.

108
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:20,780
Rinse and blot the probe and place it into the buffer.

109
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:25,100
Wait for the display to stabilize and record the pH.

110
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:29,820
Correct buffer preparation and pH meter technique are essential to ensure that your proteins

111
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:33,140
and other sensitive chemicals live long functional lives.
